ACME Corp Q4 2025 Earnings Call Transcript

CEO Sarah Chen: Good afternoon everyone, and thank you for joining our fourth
quarter and full year 2025 earnings call. I'm pleased to report that ACME Corp
delivered strong results that exceeded our guidance on both the top and bottom
line.

For Q4, revenue came in at $2.47 billion, up 18% year-over-year, driven by
robust demand in our cloud services and enterprise software segments. Full year
revenue reached $9.2 billion, representing 22% annual growth. Our gross margin
expanded 310 basis points to 71.4%, reflecting improved operational efficiency
and favorable product mix.

Let me walk through the key highlights by segment.

Cloud Services, our fastest-growing business, generated $1.1 billion in Q4
revenue, up 34% year-over-year. Annual recurring revenue (ARR) for cloud
crossed the $4 billion mark in December. Customer retention rate remained
stellar at 97%. We added 847 net new enterprise customers in the quarter,
bringing our total enterprise customer count to 12,400.

Enterprise Software delivered $980 million in Q4 revenue, up 11% year-over-year.
This segment benefited from strong demand for our AI-powered analytics suite,
which saw 58% growth. License revenue was flat, but subscription revenue grew
28%, reflecting our successful transition to a recurring revenue model.

Professional Services contributed $390 million, up 5% year-over-year, with
margins improving to 22% from 18% in the prior year period.

On the cost side, we maintained disciplined spending. Operating expenses as a
percentage of revenue declined to 52% from 56% in the prior year. R&D spending
was $520 million for the quarter, or 21% of revenue, as we continue to invest
heavily in our AI and machine learning capabilities.

Non-GAAP operating income was $480 million, representing a 19.4% operating
margin, up from 14.8% in Q4 2024. Free cash flow was $410 million for the
quarter and $1.6 billion for the full year.

CFO Michael Torres: Thank you, Sarah. Let me provide some additional financial
details and our outlook.

Our balance sheet remains strong. We ended the quarter with $3.8 billion in
cash and short-term investments, and total debt of $2.1 billion. During the
quarter, we repurchased $200 million of our common stock.

For Q1 2026 guidance, we expect revenue of $2.55 to $2.60 billion, representing
19-21% year-over-year growth. Non-GAAP operating margin is expected to be
approximately 20%. We expect non-GAAP EPS of $1.42 to $1.48.

For full year 2026, we are initiating guidance of $10.8 to $11.2 billion in
revenue, representing 17-22% growth. We expect operating margins to continue
expanding, targeting 21% for the full year.

Q&A Session:

Analyst (Morgan Stanley): Can you talk about the competitive landscape in cloud
services? Are you seeing any pricing pressure?

CEO Sarah Chen: We're actually seeing the opposite. Our differentiated AI
capabilities are allowing us to command premium pricing. Win rates against our
top three competitors improved to 62% in Q4, up from 55% a year ago. Customers
are willing to pay more for platforms that deliver measurable ROI through
AI-driven insights.

Analyst (Goldman Sachs): What's the outlook for the enterprise software
transition to subscription?

CEO Sarah Chen: We expect the subscription mix to reach 75% of enterprise
software revenue by end of 2026, up from 60% today. This transition temporarily
pressures reported revenue growth but significantly improves revenue quality
and predictability. We're very pleased with the pace of migration.

Analyst (JP Morgan): Can you provide more color on your AI investments and
expected returns?

CEO Sarah Chen: Absolutely. We're investing approximately $2 billion in AI R&D
across 2025 and 2026 combined. We've already seen tangible returns. Our
AI-powered analytics suite grew 58% this year. Our new AI copilot feature,
launched in September, already has 3,200 enterprise customers. We expect AI
related revenue to reach $2 billion by 2027, representing roughly 15-18% of
total revenue.
